Herod realized that the Magi had deceived him, and he became furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and all around it that all the boy babies who were in the age of two years or less should be killed. And this was according to the time that the Magi had seen the star.
Thus it happened as the prophet Jeremiah had said, for he had said:
A voice was heard in Ramah, lamenting and weeping and wailing. Rachel weeping for her children and refusing to be comforted because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The family of Jesus returns to Nazareth.
And after Herod died, behold, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream, while he was in Egypt.
The angel said, "Lift up your hands with the child and his mother and depart to the land of Israel. Those who were wanting to kill the child died."
So Joseph arose and took the child and his mother Mary and returned with them to the land of Israel.
But he heard that Herod's son Archelaus was king in Judea and ruled over the throne after his father Herod. Joseph was afraid to go there. In a dream he saw that he should go to Galilee region.
And there he lived in a city called Nazareth. And thus the prophecy of the prophets concerning Jesus was fulfilled. For they said: "He will be called the Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came, preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Turn away from your sins, for the Kingdom of the Heavens has drawn near."
Indeed, John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke, who said: "Behold, the voice of a crying in the wilderness: 'Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothing was made of goat hair. He girt his waist with a belt of skin. His food was locusts and wild honey.
People came out to him from Jerusalem and all Judea, and all the region around the Jordan.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the River Jordan.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to be baptized by him, so he said to them: "O offspring of vipers, who warned you to flee from the wrath to come?"
Bear fruit in keeping with repentance, so that you may demonstrate your true repentance for your sins.
Do not suppose that by saying, 'Our father is Abraham,' you will be children of Abraham. God is able to raise up children for Abraham from these stones.
The offshoot is standing on its own roots. Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repentance for your sins. But after me comes one who is mightier than I. I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
And his shepherd staff is in his hand to clear with it the straw of his threshing floor. He gathers his wheat into the granary, but as for the straw, he burns it with unquenchable fire. The Deaconhood of Jesus
Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan. He wanted John to baptize him.
But John tried to prevent him. And he said to Jesus, "I need you to baptize me, so who am I to come to me?"
Jesus answered, "This is how it must be now. It is our duty to do all that is right. Let us fulfill God's will." John agreed with this.
As soon as Jesus was baptized, he went up from the water. And at that moment, the heavens were torn open and Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness, where he was tempted by Satan.
And after forty days and nights during which Jesus ate nothing, he became hungry.
The tempter came and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become loaves of bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city and stopped him at the top of the temple.
If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down, for it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' (Psalm 91:11, 12)
Jesus answered him, "It is written again, 'You shall not tempt the Lord your God.' " (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
He said, "If you prostrate to me and worship me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Go away, Satan! For it is written, 'You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him alone you shall serve.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. And angels came and took care of him. And Jesus began to preach.
John was imprisoned. When Jesus heard about it, he returned to Galilee.
Leaving Nazareth, Jesus went and lived in Capernaum, a city by the Sea of Galilee, in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
This is what Isaiah the prophet said would happen.
The land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali! Galilee, which is in the center, the vile inhabitant dwelling therein! The land that stretches out to the Sea of Galilee, across the Jordan eastward!
Those who dwell in the darkness will see a great light. They dwell in a land of hardship and the shadows of death. But for you, the light will spread like the day, and the shadows of your death will be like the twilight.
From that time Jesus began to preach, saying, "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand." Jesus chose the first disciples.
One day Jesus was walking along the shore of the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw two brothers, Simon Peter and his brother Andrew, casting a net into the lake, for they were fishermen.
Come, follow me, said Jesus. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left the fish and followed him for a time.
And after that, he saw two others walking in the ship; they were James the son of Zebedee and his brother John, the sons of Zebedee. They were mending their nets. And he called them.
And they left the ship, their father, and followed him. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus directed his course to all of Galilee, and taught in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing all manner of sickness and all manner of disease among the people.
News about him spread throughout Syria. And people were bringing to him all the sick people afflicted with various diseases. And some of them were suffering from excruciating pains, and some were possessed by demons, and some were shaking violently, and others were completely unable to move. And Jesus healed them all.
Large crowds followed him. Some of them came from Galilee, from the region known as the Decapolis, and some from Jerusalem and Judea. Others also came from the region beyond the Jordan River.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds, so he went up into the mountain. He sat down there. His disciples approached him.
Then he began to teach them, saying:
Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are the meek and those who hunger and thirst for justice, for they shall be satisfied.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall find mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God.
Blessed are those who are persecuted for the sake of righteousness, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
The best thing for you is when people persecute you and harm you and say all kinds of lies against you that they attribute to my cause.
Rejoice and be glad, for your reward in heaven is great. This is what they did to the prophets before you. You are the salt of the earth and the light of the world.
