Look at the birds of the sky, they do not sow nor do they harvest, nor do they gather into barns, and your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much better than they?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you care about clothing? Look at how wildflowers grow. They neither toil nor do they spin.
But behold, this is what I say unto you: Never did Solomon in all his glory wear any attire to match a single one of these blossoms.
If this is the manifestation of God to the grass that springs up in the field, and does not abide but for a day, is it not probable that God would far, far better clothe you, you, who sow goodly things in fear and trembling, hoping for an eternal harvest?
Therefore do not be anxious, saying, 'What shall we eat?' or 'What shall we drink?' or 'What shall we wear?'
The infidels strive after all these things. Your heavenly Father knows that you need them.
But first seek His kingdom and His righteousness, and all these things will be added to you.
Do not worry about tomorrow, tomorrow will take care of itself. Each day has enough trouble of its own.
Be fair when judging others.
Do not judge others, lest you be judged.
You will be judged by the same standard that you judge others. You will be measured by the measuring rod you measure others by.
You look at the speck in your friend's eye. And you do not notice the log in your own eye.
How can you say to your friend, "Let me take the piece of wood out of your eye"? How can you say this when you have a piece of wood in your own eye?
