To whoever has that kind of knowledge, more will be given, in fact they will have a lot. But whoever does not have it, even the little they think they have will be taken away from them.
This is why I use parables when I speak to people. I tell them, "They look but do not really see. They listen but do not truly hear or understand."
In them shall be fulfilled the words of the prophet Isaiah, who said: 'You shall indeed hear, but understand not; and see, perceive not.'
These people's hearts have become hard, so they do not listen with their ears. They have made their eyes' sight a cover, so they do not see with it. And they have made their ears' hearing a stone, so will they not look back and face their Lord? If they were to ask Him, He would surely guide them. Woe to those whose hearts are hard against My reminder, so they do not stand up.
And blessed are your eyes because they see, and your ears because they hear.
What I am about to update you on is truly a remarkable reality. Many prophets and righteous ones have wished to see what you are seeing now. However, they were not blessed by God with that. They wished to hear what you are hearing now, but that was not for them.
Listen! This is the meaning of the peasant's story.
The people hear the message about the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the devil comes and steals what was planted in their hearts. Those people are like seed sown along the path.
And a qob, different from them, anatharat in the stones, the mostawahishah, they are yasma'oon al kalimah wa halama yasma'oonaha yafrahoon biha.
But they have no root. Therefore, they last for only a little while. When trouble or persecution comes because of the message, they quickly fall away from the faith.
And others fell among the thorns; and they grew up and choked them. As for the seed that fell on the good soil, they hear the word, accept it, and bear fruit thirty, sixty, or a hundredfold.
And the others are like seeds that fell on fertile ground. They are the ones who hear the word and understand it. These produce a crop thirty, sixty, and a hundredfold.
Jesus spoke another parable to the crowds, saying, "Thus is the kingdom of heaven compared to a man who sowed good seed in his field.
And while everyone was asleep, his enemy came. The enemy sowed grass seed in the middle of the wheat and left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
His servants came to him and said, "Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where, then, did the weeds come from?"
He said, "An enemy has done that." The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull up those weeds?"
"Impossible," replied the field owner. "For by removing the invasive weeds, you may kill the wheat as well."
He said, "Leave them both to grow together until the harvest. And at the time of the harvest, I will say to the reapers, 'Gather the first the grass, and bind it into bundles for burning. But the wheat gather and put into my barns.' " The parables of the leaven and the grain of mustard seed.
Jesus said to the crowd again in parables: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. Which when a man has taken it and sown it in his field.
It is the smallest of all your seeds, but when it grows, it becomes the biggest garden plant of all. It becomes a tree. Birds come and rest on its branches.
Jesus said to them again, in parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of flour, till the whole was leavened."
Jesus was speaking to the crowds in parables, and without parables he would never speak to them.
Jesus illustrates the parable of the tares.
Then he left the crowd and entered the house. The disciples came to him saying, "Explain to us the parable of the tares of the field of wheat."
He answered them, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man."
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who sows them is the devil. And the harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The grass is uprooted and burned in the fire. This is how it will be on the day of reckoning.
The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will remove from his kingdom all that causes sin, and they will throw all evildoers out.
They will cast them into the furnace of fire. There the people cry and gnash their teeth.
Then, the friends of God are filled with wonder in the kingdom of their Father, like the flower of the sun, radiant to the hearing ears that hear the speech of the hidden treasure and the pearl.
The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
And another time, it is similar to the Kingdom of the Heavens is like a merchant of pearls who was seeking a very precious pearl.
He found one pearl of very great value; so he went and sold all that he had, and bought that pearl of great price. The Story of the Net.
He alone also possesses the kingdom of the heavens. It is like a net that was thrown into the sea, and gathered every kind of fish.
When the net was full, the fishermen drew it to the shore. Then they sat down and took the good fish and put them in the baskets. But the bad fish they threw away.
Thus will be the Resurrection. The angels will come. They will separate those who did evil from those who did righteousness.
They cast them into the furnace of blazing fire. There the unjust weep and shriek, tearing their teeth in burning pain.
Do you understand all these things? Jesus asked them. And they answered him, Yes.
He said to them, "Every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a household who brings out from his treasure what is new and what is old." A prophet without honor.
The final words of Jesus, I follow them, his departure from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. And he began there to teach people in their religious synagogue. And they were astonished and said, "From where has this man obtained this wisdom and the ability to perform miracles?"
Isn't this the son of the carpenter? Isn't his mother called Mary? Aren't his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
None of those present accepted him at all. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country, and among his own kin he has no standing."
He didn't perform many miracles there because they were not believers.
The head of John the Baptist was cut off.
And at that time, Herod the ruler of Galilee and the region around the lake heard about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has such power to perform miracles."
Herod ordered John to be arrested, bound him, and imprisoned him because of Herodias. She was the wife of his brother Philip.
John said to Herod, "It is against our law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John the Baptist. But he was afraid of the people, because they considered him a prophet.
And on Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced before Herod and his guests. Herod was greatly pleased with her.
So he swore to her that he would give her what she was asking for.
Her mother said to her, "What are you saying?" So the girl said to King Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a large plate."
The king was very angry, but he remembered his promise and the dinner guests he had, so he told one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
They presented his head on a large platter to the girl, who then carried it to her mother.
The disciples of John came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds five thousand people.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He sought solitude. He took a boat and went to a remote place. The crowd heard and followed him on foot from the neighboring villages.
And when Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd of people, and he felt a tenderness towards them. And he healed the sick among those who were lying at his feet.
When evening came, the disciples went to him and said, "This is a remote place, and it's already late. Send the crowds away to go to the villages and buy food for themselves."
Jesus answered: "They are not obliged to leave, you give them a livelihood that they eat."
We said all we had was five loaves of bread and two fish.
Bring them here to me.
Then Jesus asked the crowds to sit on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish, and looked up to heaven, thanking God. Then he broke the bread and gave it to the disciples, who in turn gave it to the people.
Everyone ate and were satisfied. The disciples then collected the surplus, filling twelve baskets.
And the number of men who ate was about five thousand. In addition to the women and children. Jesus walks on water.
And He instructed the disciples to embark on the ship immediately and to precede Him to the other shore of the Sea of Galilee, then He dismissed the crowds.
And after he had dismissed them, he went up into the mountain to pray alone. And when evening had come, he was there alone.
The boat had already gone far from the shore; the waves were tossing it strongly because the wind was opposing it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. And He walked on the sea.
They saw him walking on the lake, and they were terrified. "A spirit!" they cried out, and they screamed in fright.
Jesus cried out to them, "Be of good cheer! I am he, do not be afraid."
"Master, are you he? asked Peter. If you are he, command me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." So Peter got out of the boat, and he walked on the water and he turned toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. And beginning to sink, he cried out, saying, "Lord, save me!"
And Jesus immediately stretched out his hand and grasped him, and said to him, "O you of little faith! Why did you doubt?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
So they submitted and said, "By the truth, you are the son of God!"
They crossed the lake and disembarked at Capernaum.
And the men who were inhabitants of that place recognized him, so they sent to all the surrounding villages and brought to him all the sick people.
They begged him to allow the sick just to touch the edge of his cloak. And all who touched it were healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some scribes came from Jerusalem to see Jesus.
Why do your pupils not practice what the elders teach? Your pupils do not wash their hands before eating!
Jesus answered: "Why do you not obey the commands of the Lord? You love to teach from your own teachings more!"
The Lord said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) And He also said, "Whoever curses his father or his mother, he will be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
And you will see many of them racing one another in sin and enmity and in devouring what is unlawful. How hateful is what they have been doing.
Therefore, they do not need to honor their parents by gifting to them. You are nullifying the word of God to make your own teachings.
O ye hypocrites! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said:
These people honor me with their words, but their hearts are far from me.
Their worship does not mean anything to me. They teach the commandments of men. — Isaiah 29:13
Jesus directed His invitation to the crowd gathered around Him, saying, "Listen and understand."
What enters into your mouth does not defile you, but what comes out of your mouth, that is what defiles you.
Then the sleeping ones drew near to him and said, "Does Jesus know that the Pharisees have heard that you are gathering this great crowd?"
Jesus answered, "There are some seeds which my heavenly Father has planted, and there are others which have been planted by others. And I tell you truly: There is coming a time when the reapers will go out to gather in this harvest, and they will pull out of the ground all that does not belong to the heavenly Father from the beginning."
Leave them alone... The Pharisees are guides for the blind... If the blind leads another blind, they will both fall into the ditch.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
Don't you understand even now? Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that proceed out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the things that make you 'defiled'.
The wicked thoughts of the heart are as murder, adultery, and sexual immorality in their forms. Likewise, theft, false witness, and slander against others.
Those are the things that make you 'unclean.' However, eating without washing hands does not make anyone unclean. The faith of the Canaanite woman.
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Sidon and Tyre.
A Canaanite woman from the land of Canaan was living near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and cried out, saying, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! For my daughter is severely possessed by a demon."
Jesus did not utter a word. The disciples approached him and begged him, saying, "Remove her from us, for she is crying out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel."
Then the woman prostrated herself before him on her knees. "Oh Lord! Help me!" she said that.
He answered and said, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
Yes, sir, that one said. But even dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table.
Then Jesus answered, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be as you want." And her daughter was healed at that hour itself. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus went away from there. And He passed over the Sea of Galilee. And He went up into a mountain. And He sat there.
Many multitudes came to Him, bringing those who were sick and those who were crippled, blind, mute, and lame; and they laid them down at His feet, and He healed them.
And a robe of bewilderment descended upon the people. Those who spoke with the word of miracles were silenced, and the paralyzed walked, and the blind saw. So the assembly glorified with the praise of the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples and said, "I have compassion on the crowd, for they have been with me now for three days and have nothing to eat. And if I send them away hungry to their homes, they will faint on the way, because many of them came from far away."
His disciples answered him, "We have no more than five loaves and two fish, unless we are to go and buy food for all these people."
How many loaves do you have? Jesus asked them. They answered him, Seven, and we have a few small fish as well.
Jesus said to the crowd, "Let the little children come to me and do not hinder them, for the Kingdom of God belongs to such as these."
And He took the seven loaves and the ears of grain, and having given thanks, He broke them and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples distributed them to the multitude.
Everyone ate and were satisfied. Then the disciples picked up seven baskets full of the leftover bread.
The number of men who ate was four thousand, and the women and children also ate.
And after Jesus had sent away the crowds, he went up on the mountain by himself. And when evening came, he was there alone. As for the boat, it had already gone to the other shore, near the land of Magadan.
Jesus is asked to give a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came, testing Jesus, and asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
He replied: "In the evening look at the sky. It says: 'The weather will be good. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning she says, "It will be windy today. The sky is red and overcast." She knows the meaning of what she sees in the sky. But you do not understand the signs of what is happening now.
A bad people, they disbelieved in the Book and in what We sent Our messengers with. So they will know, when they see the punishment, which of you is more burdensome to man. He will say, 'Indeed, it is only My Lord who delays them for a stated term. And He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.'
The pupils crossed over to the other side of the lake. They forgot to take bread with them.
Beware," Jesus told them. "Watch out for the hypocrisy of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
And the disciples talked to each other, saying, "This says this because we have not taken bread."
Jesus knew what they were thinking, so he said to them, "O little faith, why do you reason among yourselves that you have no bread?"
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you remember the five loaves that provided food for five thousand? Don't you recall how many baskets of leftovers you collected?
Weren't fifteen trips enough for the four thousand? How many shirts have you filled with crumbs that you've emptied?
How can you not understand? I did not speak to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Finally, the disciples understood that Jesus was not warning them about the leaven of bread, but about the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees. And Peter's confession of Christ as the Messiah
When Jesus went away to the regions of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They answered and said, "Some say John the Baptist; some, Elijah; and others, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"And you?" he asked them. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven."
This is what I say to you. You are Peter. And upon this rock I will build my church. And the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.
I will give you the keys to the Kingdom of the Heavens. Whatever you bind on Earth shall be bound in Heaven, and whatever you loose on Earth shall be loosed in Heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Christ. He spoke of his impending death.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what was to happen to him. He said to them, "The Son of Man must suffer greatly and be rejected by the elders, the chief priests and the teachers of the law. He will be killed and after three days will rise again."
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "God forbid, Lord!" Peter said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me; for you are not setting your mind on God's interests, but on man's."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "If anyone wants to come after me, he must deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me.
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it, but if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What good would it do if someone gained the whole world but lost their own soul? Or what can a person give in exchange for their own soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and then He will reward each one according to his works.
I am not about to tell you something that is not true. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom.
Jesus' appearance changed.
And after six days, Jesus took Peter, Jacob, and John his brother, and He led them up to a high mountain by themselves.
And his appearance changed before them, and his face shone like the sun, and his clothes became white, shining like light.
And suddenly, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. They were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three tents—one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while he was speaking with them, a white cloud opened and overshadowed them, and a voice came from the cloud saying, "This is my chosen son, listen to him."
When the disciples heard this, they were exceedingly astonished and fell to the ground upon their faces before the Lord.
And the Lord said to Moses, "See, I have made you a god to Pharaoh, and Aaron your brother shall be your mouthpiece."
And when they looked, they saw a single man alone, who was Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And while they were coming down, Jesus told them what to do. He said to them, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen, wait until the Son of Man has been raised from the dead."
And the disciples asked Jesus, "Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "What you have said is true. For it is necessary for Elijah to come first and restore all things."
But I tell you: Elijah has come, and they have done with him all they wished. So also the Son of Man will suffer at their hands.
The disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Healing of a demon-possessed boy.
And when they came near the crowd, a man approached Jesus and knelt before him.
Sir, have mercy on my son, for he suffers from violent and severe tremors, and he often falls into the fire or water.
I brought it to them for your disciples, but they could not heal him.
"O infidels and O wicked!" Jesus replied. "Until when shall I remain with you? Until when shall I tolerate you? Bring the boy to me."
Jesus commanded the devil to come out of the boy, and he came out of him that very hour. And the boy was immediately healed.
Then the disciples approached Jesus privately and asked him, "Why could we not drive out the demon?"
He answered and said, "Because of the smallness of your faith. Truly I say to you, if you have faith like a mustard seed, you will say to this mountain, 'Be removed from here to there,' and it will be done. And nothing will be impossible to you."
<.>
They met in Galilee. And Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him, and on the third day, he will rise from the dead." At this, the disciples were filled with sorrow. Jesus Pays the Temple Tax
Jesus and his disciples arrived at Capernaum. After they had rested at home, the tax collectors came to Peter and said to him, "Does your teacher not pay the temple tax?"
Yes, he does, he answered. And when Peter entered the house, it was Jesus who spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "On whom do earthly kings collect tribute or tax? From their sons or from strangers?"
Peter translated it as: "From others." Jesus answered him: "Then the two sons do not have to pay the debt."
But we don't want to anger them. Go to the lake and cast your fishing rod. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find exactly the coin you need. Take it and give it to them as a tax for you and me.
